# lib/peerframe.ad — THE PEER DOES NOT DECIDE THE SIZE OF YOUR BUFFER.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. One defect shape has been found five times in this
# tree, in five unrelated protocols, and every instance failed far from its
# cause and said nothing at the limit:
#
#   4096 vs a modern Xwayland's 8268-byte setup reply (user/wsyswl.ad) --
#        every X client froze;
#   4092 vs an X11 request that may be 262140 bytes (user/x11srv.ad) --
#        the connection desynchronised permanently;
#   255  vs an ssh pre-identification banner line (user/sshd.ad) --
#        the peer could choose where its own data was framed;
#   504  vs an X setup reply in two X clients -- alive only because the
#        peer they happen to talk to is small;
#   1 MiB vs a 2,112,018-byte blit (user/linux-wsys.c) -- no v2 window
#        larger than about 512x512 had ever painted on this port.
#
# The last was a fixed POLICY (copy-then-check) rather than a fixed buffer,
# which is why hunting the shape finds what hunting the symptom does not.
#
# THE THREE RULES, which are what every one of those sites got wrong. They are
# not new: they are what the parts of this tree that already work do, and this
# file only makes them callable.
#
#   1. THE BUFFER'S CAPACITY IS THE AUTHORITY, NEVER THE PEER'S NUMBER.
#      Parse using the count of bytes you ACTUALLY read, never the length the
#      peer declared. lib/9p/9p.ad's decoders already state this as a contract:
#      "-1 truncated input (size field promises bytes the caller hasn't
#      supplied)" -- they are handed (buf, len) and refuse when the size field
#      runs past len. That single clause is what prevents the misparse, and it
#      is the clause all five broken sites were missing.
#
#   2. WHAT DOES NOT FIT IS DRAINED, NOT ABANDONED.
#      A length-prefixed stream stays in sync only if every byte the peer
#      announced is consumed. user/xsnarfd.ad already does this: it takes what
#      fits, sets a truncation flag, and drains the remainder through a 1 KiB
#      sink. x11srv did not, and one oversized request desynchronised the
#      connection for good -- every later reply was read from the middle of an
#      earlier one.
#
#   3. SAY IT AT THE LIMIT, BY NAME.
#      Every one of the five failed at a distance from its cause: a frozen X
#      client, a blank browser, a KEX failure naming neither side. A refusal at
#      the limit that names the buffer and both numbers costs one line and is
#      the difference between a minute and a day. user/xsnarfd.ad again:
#      "this X server's maximum request is too small for the clipboard;
#      refusing rather than sending a truncated one".
#
# TWO HELPERS AND NOT ONE, AND THE REASON IS HONEST RATHER THAN TIDY. A
# length-prefixed frame and a delimiter-terminated line cannot share a reader:
# one knows how many bytes to consume before it reads them and the other only
# discovers the end by finding it. Forcing them together would mean a helper
# that takes a length OR a delimiter and ignores half its arguments, which is
# how an interface stops being read. So there are two, and they share the three
# rules above and the same shape of result: bytes taken, and a flag saying the
# peer sent more than fits.
#
# WHAT THESE HELPERS ARE FOR, AND WHAT THEY ARE NOT FOR. Read this before
# reaching for peerframe_line in a new caller; it has a range, and it is
# narrower than the rules above.
#
#   FOR: a handful of frames or lines ONCE PER CONNECTION -- an ssh
#   identification exchange, a protocol banner, a setup reply. The reads here
#   are unbuffered: _pf_read_exact issues a sys_read per call, and
#   peerframe_line calls it ONE BYTE AT A TIME because a delimiter can only be
#   found by looking at each byte as it arrives.
#
#   NOT FOR: a block-oriented reader on a hot path. MEASURED AGAINST A REAL
#   SECOND CALLER -- user/httpd_worker.ad, whose header block is up to 8 KiB --
#   peerframe_line would turn one request into about eight thousand syscalls.
#   httpd_worker reads in blocks and scans, and it was left doing that: the
#   three rules above were applied to it directly instead. THE RULES TRANSFER;
#   THIS CODE DOES NOT.
#
#   Also not for a length-prefixed frame whose length is not declared up front:
#   peerframe_take needs the count before it reads, so a delimiter-framed
#   protocol like HTTP cannot use it at all.
#
# WHAT WOULD MAKE IT GENERAL, if anyone wants that: a buffered reader
# underneath -- fill a scratch block with one sys_read, scan it for the
# delimiter, and keep the remainder for the next call. That is a real
# improvement and deliberately not done here, because the one caller that
# needed it did not need the speed and inventing an abstraction for a second
# caller before meeting one is how libraries acquire the wrong shape.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE DOES NOT COVER, stated so nobody assumes it does:
#
#   * A WRAPPED STREAM. These take a raw fd and use sys_read. A site reading
#     through TLS, a decompressor, or its own buffering layer cannot call them
#     -- it must apply the three rules itself. lib/9p/9p.ad's decode contract
#     is the model for those: pass the parser what you HAVE, not what you were
#     promised.
#   * THE C BACKENDS. user/linux-*.c cannot import an .ad module. The same
#     three rules apply there and the fix in user/linux-wsys.c's draw/ctl path
#     is what they look like in C: a complete record is parsed in place out of
#     the caller's buffer, and only a genuinely split remainder is copied into
#     a bounded reassembly buffer.
#   * A PROTOCOL THAT GENUINELY BOUNDS THE PEER. Where the peer cannot exceed
#     the buffer, no helper is needed -- but the bound must be CITED at the
#     declaration, not assumed. 9P is the example to copy: it NEGOTIATES msize
#     at mount, so user/distrofs.ad's 131072-byte buffer is exactly the msize
#     it advertised in Rversion, and the peer is contractually bound.

extern def sys_read(fd: int32, buf: Ptr[uint8], count: uint64) -> int64
extern def sys_write(fd: int32, buf: Ptr[uint8], count: uint64) -> int64

# The drain sink. One kilobyte, reused: the bytes are being thrown away, so the
# only thing that matters is that they are consumed from the stream.
PEERFRAME_SINK_CAP: uint64 = 1024
_pf_sink: Array[1024, uint8]


def _pf_read_exact(fd: int32, buf: Ptr[uint8], n: uint64) -> int32:
    # Read exactly n bytes. 0 on success, -1 if the peer went away. A short
    # read is not an error on a stream socket -- it is the ordinary case -- so
    # this loops rather than trusting one call, which is its own small instance
    # of the same lesson.
    got: uint64 = 0
    while got < n:
        k: int64 = sys_read(fd, cast[Ptr[uint8]](cast[uint64](buf) + got), n - got)
        if k <= 0:
            return -1
        got = got + cast[uint64](k)
    return 0


def peerframe_drain(fd: int32, n: uint64) -> int32:
    # Consume and discard exactly n bytes. THIS IS RULE 2. Skipping it is what
    # turns one oversized frame into a permanently desynchronised connection.
    left: uint64 = n
    while left > 0:
        chunk: uint64 = left
        if chunk > PEERFRAME_SINK_CAP:
            chunk = PEERFRAME_SINK_CAP
        if _pf_read_exact(fd, &_pf_sink[0], chunk) != 0:
            return -1
        left = left - chunk
    return 0


def peerframe_take(fd: int32, buf: Ptr[uint8], cap: uint64, declared: uint64,
                   out_taken: Ptr[uint64], out_over: Ptr[int32]) -> int32:
    # A LENGTH-PREFIXED FRAME whose length the peer chose.
    #
    #   out_taken  how many bytes are in `buf` -- THE ONLY NUMBER THE CALLER
    #              MAY PARSE WITH. Never parse with `declared`.
    #   out_over   1 if the peer sent more than fits. The frame in `buf` is a
    #              PREFIX of a larger one; a caller that cannot act on a prefix
    #              must refuse it rather than parse it (rule 1).
    #
    # Returns 0 on success, -1 if the peer went away. The stream is left
    # correctly positioned at the start of the next frame in BOTH cases where
    # it returns 0 -- that is the whole point of the drain.
    taken: uint64 = declared
    over: int32 = 0
    if taken > cap:
        taken = cap
        over = 1
    out_taken[0] = taken
    out_over[0] = over
    if taken > 0:
        if _pf_read_exact(fd, buf, taken) != 0:
            return -1
    if declared > taken:
        if peerframe_drain(fd, declared - taken) != 0:
            return -1
    return 0


def peerframe_line(fd: int32, buf: Ptr[uint8], cap: uint64, delim: uint8,
                   out_len: Ptr[uint64], out_over: Ptr[int32]) -> int32:
    # A DELIMITER-TERMINATED LINE whose length the peer chose.
    #
    # The whole line is consumed to `delim` however long it is; at most `cap`
    # bytes are kept. out_over is 1 when the line was longer than cap, and an
    # over-long line MUST NOT be accepted as a frame -- user/sshd.ad records
    # what happens when it is: the remainder of one physical line gets treated
    # as the start of the next, so the peer chooses where its own data is
    # framed. There is no NUL terminator written here; out_len is the length.
    #
    # Returns 0 on success, -1 if the peer went away before the delimiter.
    n: uint64 = 0
    over: int32 = 0
    c: Array[1, uint8]
    while True:
        if _pf_read_exact(fd, &c[0], 1) != 0:
            return -1
        if c[0] == delim:
            break
        if n < cap:
            buf[n] = c[0]
            n = n + 1
        else:
            over = 1
    out_len[0] = n
    out_over[0] = over
    return 0
